# Postgres in {{ .Name }}

`postgres.Enable(postgres.WithMigrations(migrations.FS))` in `cmd/{{ .Name }}/main.go`
declares the pool; `postgres.From(a)` returns it, a pgx v5 `*pgxpool.Pool`. Write SQL
with pgx: no ORM, no query builder. Recipes `add-migration`, `add-postgres-query`
and `paginate-a-list` (`docs/recipes/` in the gox module directory) show complete
code.

## Change the schema

1. Add `migrations/<NNNNNN>_<change>.up.sql` and `.down.sql`, numbered after the
   highest file there (the first is `000001_init`). `//go:embed *.sql` in
   `migrations/migrations.go` picks them up; no Go change.
2. They run at boot, before `/readyz` reports ready, under a lock, so replicas
   starting together are safe. `{{ .Prefix }}_POSTGRES_MIGRATE=false` skips them where a
   deploy step runs `postgres.Migrate(ctx, url, migrations.FS)` instead.
3. Each file runs as one transaction: a failing statement rolls back the whole
   file. `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` cannot run in a transaction: give it a file of
   its own.
4. Never edit, rename or delete a migration that has run anywhere: it never runs
   again there, and boot fails once the file of the database's version is missing.
   Add a new one.
5. A failed migration marks the database dirty, and every boot after fails with
   `Dirty database version N. Fix and force version.` On a local database: fix the
   file, set the row back to the previous file's number,
   `UPDATE schema_migrations SET version = <previous>, dirty = false` (the failed
   file rolled back; delete the row if it was the first file), and boot again.

## Query

- The pool reaches handlers through `Register`, never through `postgres.From` in a
  handler: `invoices.Register(a, postgres.From(a))` in main, kept in the handler
  struct (`type handler struct{ db *pgxpool.Pool }`).
- Pass `r.Context()` to every query. Values go in `$1` placeholders, never into the
  SQL string.
- Map the two expected failures to gox errors, and let everything else become a
  500 whose cause is logged:

```go
err := h.db.QueryRow(r.Context(), "SELECT id, number FROM invoices WHERE id = $1", id).Scan(&inv.ID, &inv.Number)
if errors.Is(err, pgx.ErrNoRows) {
	gox.Error(w, r, gox.NotFound("invoice %s", id))
	return
}

var pgErr *pgconn.PgError
if errors.As(err, &pgErr) && pgErr.Code == "23505" { // unique_violation
	gox.Error(w, r, gox.AlreadyExists("invoice number %s is taken", in.Number))
	return
}
if err != nil {
	gox.Error(w, r, err) // 500 "internal error"; never copy err.Error() into a gox message
	return
}
```

- Transactions: `postgres.Tx(ctx, pool, func(tx pgx.Tx) error { ... })` commits when
  the function returns nil and rolls back on an error or a panic; inside it, use
  `tx`, not the pool. `postgres.TxWith` also sets values that triggers and
  row-level security read with `current_setting()`.
- Imports: `github.com/jackc/pgx/v5` (`pgx.ErrNoRows`, `pgx.Tx`), `.../pgx/v5/pgconn`
  (`PgError`), `.../pgx/v5/pgxpool` (`Pool`). Run `go mod tidy` after the first one.
- Lists paginate by keyset with `gox.ParsePage`, `page.Cursor` and
  `gox.EncodeCursor`, not OFFSET: recipe `paginate-a-list`.

## Test

- Handler tests call `Register(a, nil)`: paths that fail before the database (bad
  JSON, validation) need no pool. Copy `internal/hello/handler_test.go`.
- Database tests live in `*_integration_test.go` files that start with
  `//go:build integration`, skip when `DATABASE_URL` is unset, run
  `postgres.Migrate(ctx, url, migrations.FS)`, then build the pool with
  `pgxpool.New(ctx, url)` and pass it to `Register`. Point `DATABASE_URL` at a
  throwaway database the tests own.
- Run them with `DATABASE_URL=... go test -race -tags integration ./...`. Without
  the variable they skip and still pass: check `-v` output for SKIP. `make test`
  and `make lint` leave these files out; lint them with
  `golangci-lint run --build-tags integration ./...`.

## Run it

`{{ .Prefix }}_POSTGRES_URL` is required and nothing loads `.env`:
`export {{ .Prefix }}_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://...` before `make run`. `go run ./cmd/{{ .Name }} --help`
lists the other `{{ .Prefix }}_POSTGRES_*` variables.
